# Each branch matches only the change sense, because Go doc prose uses
# the same words for runtime sequence far more often.
#
# `now VERB` requires a subject word before `now`, so `time.Now returns`,
# a sentence-initial `Now returns`, and `deadline after now uses` pass
# while `The parser now supports tables` fires.
#
# `can now` and `now also` count anywhere (`the client can now retry`,
# `the parser now also reads tables`). `is now` and `now has` stay
# out, because a test comment narrates runtime state with them (`the
# row is now consumed`, `the store now has two rows`).
#
# `previously` fires only as `had previously`, as the sentence opener
# `Previously,`, or before the subject of a clause (`Previously the
# walker visited aliases twice`). In `the previously cached value`, the
# word orders events at runtime. `was previously` and `were previously`
# count only before a word that names or ranks a feature, such as
# `named`, `hidden`, or `deprecated` (`the field was previously named
# port`). Before any other word they describe runtime state (`the
# connections that were previously connected`).
#
# Code comments that narrate the edit that produced them (`Before this
# change, the walker visited aliases twice`, `Updated to use the
# cached index`) belong in the commit message. A passive form
# (`was refactored to`) also draws the passive rule's finding.
#
# `than before` compares the system with an earlier version of itself.
# `than previously` stays out, because a Go doc uses it for a value set
# earlier at run time (`larger than previously set with
# SetMaxReadFrameSize`). `the old` and `the previous` count after
# `than` or `unlike` only before a word that names a version, because
# Go doc prose uses them for runtime order (`unlike the previous call`,
# `greater than the old value`).
#
# `the new` is limited to nouns that name a change surface, because
# `returns the new count` and `validate the new config` are ordinary
# runtime prose. `no longer`, `used to be`, and `has been updated` are
# left out: docs use them for state changes in time (`is no longer
# valid after Close`, `records what the limit used to be`, `wait until
# the row has been updated`), and the change sense of `has been updated`
# is a passive the passive rule already catches.
#
# It hit 66 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 13 of 20 sampled hits
# narrated a change. Most misses were a tutorial's progress (`You can
# now conquer the world with Nix`) or a back-reference (`As described
# previously,`), which the back-reference rule also reports.
expr versions [version, versions, release, releases, behavior, behaviour, implementation, API, default, defaults, format]
expr surfaces [flag, option, field, argument, parameter, subcommand, endpoint, syntax, format, default, behavior, behaviour, setting, mode]
expr named [named, called, known, renamed, hidden, deprecated, experimental, required, optional]
expr main [([NOUN, PROPN, PRON, DET, ADJ, NUM] now [supports, accepts, returns, uses, handles, requires, defaults, includes]), ([can, does] now), (now also), ([was, were] previously @named), (had previously), (previously,), (previously [DET, PRON, PROPN]), ([before, after, with] this change), ([updated, changed] to use), (refactored to), (the new @surfaces), (than before), ([than, unlike] the [old, previous] @versions)]
let message "Change narration. Describe the system as it is, and move the change to the commit message."
let description "Flags phrases that describe the system as a change from an earlier version."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The flag defaults to five."
allows "The parser supports tables."
allows "Set returns the new count."
allows "The value must be greater than the old value."
allows "Unlike the previous call, Close returns an error."
allows "The entry is no longer valid after Close."
allows "The key used to sign tokens lives in the secrets store."
allows "Now returns the current local time."
allows "The retry uses the previously cached value."
allows "The row is now consumed."
allows "The deadline is now plus five seconds."
allows "The watcher fires only after the file has been updated on disk."
allows "The value previously stored under the key is returned."
allows "The call fails unless the entry was previously removed."
